+**Why there is no contract filter (resolved AUG-014).** The first draft of this pseudocode read
+`D.shared_contract == answer's contract` — but `shared_contract` exists in no table. The model of
+section 1 has the contract on `bundle.contract` (one path per bundle), and a decision carries only
+`scope`. Three readings were considered:
+
+(a) add `decision.shared_contract` — a per-decision copy of a bundle-level string: denormalised,
+    drifts from the bundle row, and a decision with no bundle row cannot name one;
+(b) key the walk on `bundle.contract` — filter decisions by the bundle whose contract the answer
+    shares. But a decision row does not name its bundle either (membership is a table; a project
+    may belong to several bundles), so this is the same shape as (a) with an extra join; and it
+    would silently drop every bundle whose contract string is empty (B-02 has none), even though
+    its members are exactly the ones who need deduping.
+(c) drop the clause — the walk compares the answer against **every** bundle-scoped decision of
+    each sibling.
+
+The engine implements (c), and the spec records it as the intended shape. It stays finite by the
+same two rules as before (bundle-scoped only; one JEV call per sibling decision, not per contract
+pair), and the granularity the contract filter wanted is achieved where it actually belongs: the
+JUDGE reads both decisions' chosen text and reasons (see `bundle_impact_verdict`), so a
+decision that has nothing to do with the contract comes back "unchanged" and writes no edge. If
+a future model shows the cost is real, the honest extension is `decision.bundle_id` (which bundle
+the decision constrains) — a new field and a migration, not a re-read of this clause.
+
 Two rules keep it finite and honest:
 
